---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const evidence = [
  ["Documented association","Some mass strandings of beaked whales have occurred during or shortly after naval mid-frequency active sonar activity. Timing alone is not proof, but repeated co-occurrence is scientifically important."],
  ["Pathology and mechanism","Post-mortem studies have reported hemorrhage and gas/fat embolic lesions in affected beaked whales. Altered diving behavior and nitrogen dynamics are proposed mechanisms; the precise sequence is not universal or completely resolved."],
  ["Case-level conclusions","The strength of evidence differs by event. Some investigations link naval activity and strandings; in others sonar is investigated and excluded by chronology or other evidence."],
  ["Species pattern","Deep-diving beaked whales appear unusually represented in well-known sonar-associated events. That does not mean every beaked-whale stranding is sonar-related or that all cetaceans respond identically."],
  ["Mitigation","Operational measures include visual and acoustic monitoring, shutdown or delay zones, geographic restrictions, environmental review, and biologically important areas."],
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Sonar & whale strandings" description="A neutral evidence review of naval sonar, beaked-whale strandings, proposed mechanisms, uncertainties, counterexamples, and mitigation.">
  <section class="container archive-hero"><p class="eyebrow">Evidence review</p><h1>Sonar and whale strandings.</h1><p>The accurate version is neither “sonar never matters” nor “sonar makes whales go crazy.” Evidence varies by species, signal, event, timing, pathology, and the quality of monitoring before and after an exercise.</p><p class="verification-stamp"><time datetime="2026-09-26">Evidence last reviewed September 26, 2026</time></p></section>
  <section class="container sonar-findings"><article><p class="eyebrow">Canary Islands · 2002</p><h2>Pathology supported a sonar-associated event.</h2><p>Fourteen beaked whales stranded near an international naval exercise. A peer-reviewed veterinary study reported gas bubble-associated lesions, fat embolism, congestion, and hemorrhage, and proposed mechanisms involving altered diving behavior and nitrogen supersaturation.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16006604/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the study ↗</a></article><article><p class="eyebrow">Mariana Archipelago · 2007–2019</p><h2>A significant pattern—with a counterexample.</h2><p>NOAA researchers found several beaked-whale strandings during or within six days of anti-submarine activities. One 2019 event was also near a training event, but the Navy confirmed sonar began after the stranding.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://repository.library.noaa.gov/view/noaa/26176" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the study record ↗</a></article><article><p class="eyebrow">Current operations</p><h2>Risk is managed, not treated as imaginary.</h2><p>Navy environmental documents describe visual and acoustic monitoring, shutdown procedures, geographic limits, biological-area review, and Marine Mammal Protection Act processes.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://www.nepa.navy.mil/Completed-Projects/SURTASS-LFA-Sonar/preventive-measures/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Review mitigation ↗</a></article></section>
  <section class="container evidence-scale">{evidence.map(([title,body]) => <div class="evidence-row"><strong>{title}</strong><p>{body}</p></div>)}</section>
  <section class="container research-ledger"><div><p class="eyebrow">Limits</p><h2>What the evidence cannot honestly support.</h2></div><ul><li>Not every stranding near a naval exercise was caused by sonar.</li><li>Absence of a recovered carcass or complete acoustic record limits what can be concluded.</li><li>Gas found after death must be distinguished from gas formed in vivo; decomposition complicates interpretation.</li><li>Associations established for particular beaked whales and sonar regimes should not be generalized to every whale, sound source, or ocean.</li></ul></section>
</BaseLayout>
